---
import DocsLayout from "../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
---

<DocsLayout
  docKey="library"
  title="Your Library and backups"
  description="What the Deptheos Library holds, where it is, backing it up and restoring it, Recently Deleted, and what Delete Library deletes."
>
  <div class="prose">
    <h1>Your Library and backups</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Everything you make in Deptheos is kept in its Library: every render, its settings and its retouching.
      Your frames are not in it, and are never changed. This page says what the Library holds, where it is, how
      to back it up, and what deleting does.
    </p>
  </div>

  <section id="holds">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>What the Library holds</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Each render:</strong> its result, saved without loss, its depth map, a thumbnail, the list of
          frames it was made from with which were included, the method and settings, and any retouching painted
          on it.
        </li>
        <li><strong>Each stack:</strong> its name, its renders' numbering, and the frames and settings you were editing.</li>
        <li><strong>Failed stacks</strong> from a batch, so you can try them again.</li>
        <li><strong>Recently Deleted:</strong> what you deleted in the last 30 days (below).</li>
        <li>
          <strong>Where your frames are:</strong> the folders the renders' frames were read from. A render names
          its frames; it doesn't hold a copy of them.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Renders take room, since each holds a full-size result. The Library is kept apart from your photos, in
        the app's own folder:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>macOS: <code>~/Library/Application Support/Deptheos</code></li>
        <li>Windows: <code>%LOCALAPPDATA%\Deptheos</code></li>
        <li>Linux: <code>~/.local/share/Deptheos</code></li>
        <li>iPhone and iPad: inside the app's own storage, which Files doesn't show</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        On a Mac, Windows or Linux you can keep it on another disk: choose a folder under Settings ▸ Library ▸
        <em>Library folder</em>, and Deptheos uses it the next time it starts. A folder that iCloud Drive,
        Dropbox, OneDrive or Google Drive keeps in sync is refused, since a sync service rewriting the Library's
        files while Deptheos writes them can damage it. A Library on a drive that isn't connected waits, and
        Deptheos says to connect it. One copy of the app uses a Library at a time.
      </p>
      <p>
        On the desktop, the default folder also holds a cache of RAW files converted to DNG (<code>DNGCache</code>),
        which Deptheos makes again when it needs one, and on Windows the logs. Neither is part of the Library.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="frames">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Your frames are never changed</h2>
      <p>
        Deptheos only ever reads your frames. It never changes, moves, renames or deletes them, wherever they
        are, and nothing you do in the Library touches them: deleting a render, a stack or the whole Library
        leaves them as they were. RAW files are converted into the app's own cache, not beside your photos. An
        export is always a new file: when the name is taken, the new file gets a number rather than replacing
        the one there.
      </p>
      <p>
        On iPhone and iPad, frames you add from Files or Photos are read where they are, or copied into the
        app's Imports folder when they must be (On My iPhone ▸ Deptheos in Files). Camera bursts are saved in
        its Captures folder.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="backup">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Backing up the Library</h2>
      <p>
        Back up the Library folder as you would any folder, with Time Machine, File History or a copy on another
        drive. To make a copy by hand, quit Deptheos first, so nothing is being written while it is copied.
      </p>
      <p>
        Back up your frames too. A render shows and exports without them, but stacking again, retouching from a
        frame and comparing with a frame need the frames, and the Library holds no copy of them.
      </p>
      <p>
        To restore, quit Deptheos and put the Library folder back where it was, or anywhere and choose it under
        Settings ▸ Library ▸ <em>Library folder</em>. If your frames are no longer where they were, the Frames
        list marks them Missing and <a href="/docs/concepts/#locate">Locate…</a> points the renders at where
        they are now.
      </p>
      <p>
        A newer version of Deptheos may upgrade the Library as it opens it, and an older version then refuses
        that Library whole, without changing it. If you might go back to an older version, keep a copy from
        before you update.
      </p>
      <p>
        On iPhone and iPad, the Library and the camera's bursts are in the device's iCloud or computer backup
        with the rest of the app's data. Copies in Imports are left out of it, since their originals are in
        Photos or Files already.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="deleted">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Recently Deleted</h2>
      <p>
        Deleting a render, a stack or a failed stack doesn't ask first, and doesn't remove it at once: it moves
        to <em>Recently Deleted</em>, and the notice that says so has <em>Undo</em>. Recently Deleted, at the
        foot of the Library's sidebar on the desktop and on iPad, and at the end of the Library on iPhone, lists each delete
        with its days left and its size. <em>Restore</em> puts it back as it was, with its retouching and its
        number.
      </p>
      <p>
        Each item is kept for 30 days, then removed for good the next time Deptheos opens the Library.
        <em>Empty Recently Deleted…</em> removes everything in it now, after asking. While they are kept, deleted
        renders still take their room on disk.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="delete-library">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Delete Library</h2>
      <p>
        <em>Delete Library…</em> (Settings ▸ Library on the desktop, Settings ▸ Storage on iPhone and iPad)
        deletes every stack and render, with their retouching, the failed stacks, the working version and
        everything in Recently Deleted, leaving the Library empty. It asks first, saying how many stacks and
        renders go, and it can't be undone.
      </p>
      <p>
        It leaves your frames and the files you exported alone. On iPhone and iPad it leaves the copies in
        Imports too: Settings ▸ Storage lists those no stack uses any more, to delete separately.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</DocsLayout>
